  <h1>What Is a Cut-off?</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> A cut-off is the analyte concentration threshold that separates a positive (reactive) result from a negative (non-reactive) result. Samples at or above the cut-off read positive; below it, negative.</p>

  <h2>How a cut-off works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The test is designed so the visible line appears only when the analyte concentration reaches the cut-off.</li>
    <li>Example: a THC urine test with a 50 ng/mL cut-off is positive only when THC metabolite is ≥ 50 ng/mL.</li>
    <li>Cut-offs are set per analyte and often align with regulatory standards (e.g., SAMHSA-style workplace thresholds).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The sensitivity/specificity trade-off</h2>
  <p>The cut-off balances two kinds of error:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lower cut-off</strong> — catches more true cases (higher sensitivity) but more false positives.</li>
    <li><strong>Higher cut-off</strong> — fewer false positives (higher specificity) but more false negatives.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Buyers must confirm the cut-off matches the intended use — a screening cut-off differs from a confirmation threshold.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>The cut-off determines the test's clinical meaning. A "positive" is only meaningful relative to its threshold, which is why cut-off values appear on datasheets and why regulatory programs specify them.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What is a cut-off value?</strong> The analyte concentration threshold that separates positive from negative results.</p>
    <p><strong>Can a cut-off be changed?</strong> Yes — OEM/ODM formulations can be customized to a target cut-off for a specific market or program.</p>
    <p><strong>Does a lower cut-off mean a better test?</strong> Not necessarily — it trades sensitivity against specificity, so the right cut-off depends on the intended use.</p>
  </div>

